<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="UDP-α-D-galacturonate biosynthesis I (from UDP-D-glucuronate)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4861" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; In plants, |FRAME: D-GALACTURONATE| (GalA) is a major sugar residue of polysaccharides |CITS:[MOHNEN02]| and a minor component of some plant arabinogalactan proteins |CITS:[DARVILL80],[8727785]|. In bacteria, GalA is found in various cell-surface polysaccharides such as the O-antigens of the Gram-negative bacteria |FRAME: TAX-1162729| and |FRAME: TAX-45888| |CITS:[14670712]|. The incorporation of GalA into those polysaccharides is performed by transfer of the GalA residue from its activated nucleotide-sugar form |FRAME: UDP-D-GALACTURONATE| (UDP-GalA). This nucleotide sugar is formed through the conversion of |FRAME: UDP-GLUCURONATE| (UDP-GlcA) to UDP-GalA by a nucleotide-sugar 4-epimerase |CITS: [15563616]|. For some plants, the |FRAME:PWY-4841| pathway was shown to be a major source of UDP-GalA |CITS:[LOEWUS82]|. However, &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol is not always a quantitatively important source of carbon for cell wall uronide and pentose units |CITS:[LOEWUS82]|.</Description>
  <Comment source="Automated Conversion">This GPML file was automatically generated from PlantCyc flatfiles.</Comment>
